WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.640
Which character in "The Batman" could turn&nbsp;
out to be the hero's stalwart sidekick, Robin?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.680
Is he hiding in plain sight? Keep watching&nbsp;
for the fan theories that say just that.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.920
Batman and his major villains, supporting&nbsp;
cast, gadgets, and methods are a known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.960
quantity for moviegoers by now. But it seems like&nbsp;
bringing Batman's famous sidekick to the screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.360
is more of a challenge for many filmmakers.&nbsp;
Despite the fact that Robin is an immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:28.160
identifiable pop culture icon, his time on&nbsp;
the big screen has been relatively scarce.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.200
Christopher Nolan's trilogy of Batman films, for&nbsp;
example, only included Robin in the form of Joseph&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:37.760
Gordon-Levitt's character, whose real name is&nbsp;
revealed at the end of "The Dark Knight Rises."

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:38.720
"Try my legal name."

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.840
"You should use your full&nbsp;
name. I like that name. Robin."

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.680
Director Joel Schumacher included the character&nbsp;
in "Batman Forever," and "Batman and Robin,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:55.920
but since then, the most we've seen of this&nbsp;
brightly-garbed, orphaned circus acrobat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.480
has been a monument to his off-screen death&nbsp;
in "Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice."

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.120
This exclusion of Robin continues in "The Batman."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.160
It's arguably the grimmest and most serious big&nbsp;
screen take on Batman yet, and it can be a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.760
hard to imagine a swashbuckling Robin in it. But&nbsp;
with future sequels almost certainly on the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.920
fans are already speculating about how&nbsp;
Matt Reeves might find a way to link Robin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.080
up to this brooding version of Batman,&nbsp;
particularly given the dark new twists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:27.280
already given to characters like Riddler and the&nbsp;
Penguin. Some say he's already been introduced.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.160
If you've seen "The Batman," you remember Gotham&nbsp;
Mayor Mitchell, the Riddler's first victim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.480
and his grieving son. Bruce Wayne obviously&nbsp;
feels a connection to the boy's grief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:40.640
as it mirrors his own following the traumatic&nbsp;
death of his parents. That alone doesn't make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.560
the younger Mitchell a prospective Robin,&nbsp;
but as one fan on Reddit points out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:49.600
there are other potential clues pointing to the&nbsp;
kid's possible future as a caped crimefighter:

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.160
"I mean, they showed him multiple&nbsp;
times throughout the movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:56.800
Bruce felt connected with him for being an&nbsp;
orphan. The kid was wearing a RED ninja suit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:01.200
and wielded a katana at the beginning... I&nbsp;
mean come on. Just a little resemblance?"

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.840
While the classic Dick Grayson version of&nbsp;
Robin isn't known to fight with a katana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:10.320
the Damian Wayne version does. The stronger&nbsp;
clue, though, is the kid wearing red.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.640
Others see the character as merely a reflection&nbsp;
of Bruce Wayne, in keeping with the story's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.520
theme of Batman on an unbreakable cycle&nbsp;
of violence and grief. One user wrote:

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:23.520
"I really just took it as that kid is a&nbsp;
reflection of what happened to Bruce Wayne.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.000
The cycle is repeating itself – [B]atman&nbsp;
wasn't stopping it in his current form."

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.840
If Mitchell's son isn't Robin, maybe&nbsp;
someone else is. There's a new recruit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:36.000
to the gang seen early in the film who seems&nbsp;
visibly uncomfortable with the actions of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.200
others, and even hesitates to&nbsp;
beat up an innocent victim.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.840
He ends up running away from Batman rather&nbsp;
than fighting. Notably, he wears a red hood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:48.240
and the movie focuses on him just enough to&nbsp;
make him stick out in the memory of viewers.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:52.320
The character is played by actor Jay&nbsp;
Lycurgo, who is, probably by coincidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.800
now playing Tim Drake, the third&nbsp;
Robin, on HBO Max's "Titans." Since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.480
"Titans" definitely doesn't fit into&nbsp;
the same continuity as "The Batman,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:04.560
it would seem unlikely that both franchises&nbsp;
would have the same actor playing Robin.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.760
If the younger Mitchell doesn't end&nbsp;
up accepting the mantle of Robin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.600
nor the unnamed gang member, it wouldn't&nbsp;
be the first time that Batman fans would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:15.120
be disappointed by what they thought&nbsp;
was a clue about future characters.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.120
Remember the character of Coleman Reese in&nbsp;
"The Dark Knight"? He's the Wayne Industries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:22.800
employee who attempts to blackmail the&nbsp;
billionaire playboy with information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.680
about his secret identity. At the time, there&nbsp;
was lots of fan speculation that, because "Mr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.240
Reese" sounds a lot like "mysteries," he would&nbsp;
be the Riddler in a future film. He wasn't.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.080
"They're tryin' to kill me."

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:36.000
"Well, maybe Batman can save you."

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.760
On the other hand, all those theories about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:41.840
Barry Keoghan's role in "The Batman"&nbsp;
were proven true, so you never know.

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:45.920
Young Mitchell's red Halloween costume and&nbsp;
personal tragedy could be a red herring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:50.080
or maybe he will be Robin. Either way,&nbsp;
for now, it's fun to speculate on whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:54.400
or not such small details of "The Batman"&nbsp;
could be hints about developments later on.

